Summary of why AsyncTask is deprecated in API 30+
- It encourages background work that is tightly coupled to an Activity/Fragment lifecycle, which can easily cause memory leaks and crashes if the UI is destroyed mid-task.
- Its threading model is inflexible and easy to misuse (e.g., serial vs. parallel execution changes across API levels, weak cancellation semantics).
- It’s hard to test and doesn’t provide robust error handling or lifecycle-awareness.
- Modern alternatives (Executor/Thread + Handler, coroutines, WorkManager) give clearer control over threading, lifecycle, and scheduling.

Replacement approach for your use case
Because you’re triggering a one-off SSH command from a button press while the app is in the foreground, a simple ExecutorService (background thread) plus posting results back to the main thread is the most straightforward and efficient replacement. WorkManager is better for deferrable, guaranteed background work that should run even if the app/process dies; for a UI-triggered, immediate command, ExecutorService is appropriate.

Example Java code using ExecutorService + Handler (JSch for SSH)

Manifest (required)
- Add INTERNET permission if not already present:
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

MainActivity.java
- This replaces Soket_AsyncTask with a background Runnable. It preserves wifiModuleIp, wifiModulePort, and CMD and shows where the SSH happens.

```java
import android.os.Bundle;
import android.os.Handler;
import android.os.Looper;
import android.view.View;
import android.widget.Button;
import android.widget.EditText;
import android.widget.Toast;

import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;

import com.jcraft.jsch.ChannelExec;
import com.jcraft.jsch.JSch;
import com.jcraft.jsch.JSchException;
import com.jcraft.jsch.Session;

import java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Properties;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {

    // Existing fields you use
    public static String wifiModuleIp;
    public static int wifiModulePort;
    public static String CMD;

    private Button btnUp, btnDown;
    private EditText txtAddress;

    // Background execution
    private ExecutorService sshExecutor;
    private Handler mainHandler;

    // Example credentials; do NOT hard-code in production
    private static final String SSH_USERNAME = "pi";
    private static final String SSH_PASSWORD = "yourStrongPassword"; // Prefer key-based auth; see notes.

    private Future<?> currentTask; // to cancel if needed

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

        btnUp = findViewById(R.id.btnUp);
        btnDown = findViewById(R.id.btnDown);
        txtAddress = findViewById(R.id.ipAddress);

        sshExecutor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
        mainHandler = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());

        btnUp.setOnClickListener(new View.OnClickListener() {
            @Override
            public void onClick(View v) {
                // Set the Raspberry Pi's IP and SSH port
                MainActivity.wifiModuleIp = "192.168.1.100";
                MainActivity.wifiModulePort = 22;

                // Command to execute on the Raspberry Pi
                MainActivity.CMD = "python /path/to/script.py";

                // Kick off the SSH command off the main thread
                startSshCommand(MainActivity.wifiModuleIp, MainActivity.wifiModulePort, SSH_USERNAME, SSH_PASSWORD, MainActivity.CMD);
            }
        });

        // Example for btnDown, if needed
        btnDown.setOnClickListener(new View.OnClickListener() {
            @Override
            public void onClick(View v) {
                MainActivity.wifiModuleIp = "192.168.1.100";
                MainActivity.wifiModulePort = 22;
                MainActivity.CMD = "python /path/to/another_script.py";
                startSshCommand(MainActivity.wifiModuleIp, MainActivity.wifiModulePort, SSH_USERNAME, SSH_PASSWORD, MainActivity.CMD);
            }
        });
    }

    private void startSshCommand(final String host, final int port, final String username, final String password, final String cmd) {
        // Cancel any previous task if needed
        if (currentTask != null && !currentTask.isDone()) {
            currentTask.cancel(true);
        }

        currentTask = sshExecutor.submit(new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                SshResult result;
                try {
                    result = runSshCommand(host, port, username, password, cmd);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    result = SshResult.fromError(e);
                }

                final SshResult finalResult = result;
                mainHandler.post(new Runnable() {
                    @Override
                    public void run() {
                        if (finalResult.error != null) {
                            Toast.makeText(MainActivity.this, "SSH error: " + finalResult.error.getMessage(), Toast.LENGTH_LONG).show();
                        } else {
                            String msg = "Exit: " + finalResult.exitCode + "\n" +
                                         "STDOUT: " + finalResult.stdout + "\n" +
                                         "STDERR: " + finalResult.stderr;
                            Toast.makeText(MainActivity.this, msg, Toast.LENGTH_LONG).show();
                        }
                    }
                });
            }
        });
    }

    private SshResult runSshCommand(String host, int port, String username, String password, String cmd)
            throws JSchException, IOException, InterruptedException {

        JSch jsch = new JSch();

        // Recommended: verify host keys. Example: load known_hosts from assets or file.
        // jsch.setKnownHosts(getFilesDir().getAbsolutePath() + "/known_hosts");
        // If you must bypass temporarily (not recommended):
        Properties config = new Properties();
        config.put("StrictHostKeyChecking", "no"); // Prefer "yes" with known_hosts; see notes.

        Session session = jsch.getSession(username, host, port);
        session.setConfig(config);

        // Prefer key-based auth in production:
        // jsch.addIdentity("/path/to/private_key");
        // If using password:
        session.setPassword(password);

        // Connect with timeout
        session.connect(10000);

        ChannelExec channel = (ChannelExec) session.openChannel("exec");
        channel.setCommand(cmd);

        ByteArrayOutputStream stdout = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
        ByteArrayOutputStream stderr = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
        channel.setOutputStream(stdout);
        channel.setErrStream(stderr);

        channel.connect();

        // Wait for command completion
        while (!channel.isClosed()) {
            if (Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
                // Attempt to cancel gracefully
                try {
                    channel.disconnect();
                } catch (Exception ignored) { }
                try {
                    session.disconnect();
                } catch (Exception ignored) { }
                throw new InterruptedException("SSH task interrupted");
            }
            Thread.sleep(100);
        }

        int exitStatus = channel.getExitStatus();

        channel.disconnect();
        session.disconnect();

        return new SshResult(exitStatus, stdout.toString("UTF-8"), stderr.toString("UTF-8"), null);
    }

    private static class SshResult {
        final int exitCode;
        final String stdout;
        final String stderr;
        final Exception error;

        SshResult(int exitCode, String stdout, String stderr, Exception error) {
            this.exitCode = exitCode;
            this.stdout = stdout;
            this.stderr = stderr;
            this.error = error;
        }

        static SshResult fromError(Exception e) {
            return new SshResult(-1, "", "", e);
        }
    }

    @Override
    protected void onDestroy() {
        super.onDestroy();
        if (sshExecutor != null) {
            sshExecutor.shutdownNow();
        }
    }
}
```

What changed vs. AsyncTask
- Instead of task.execute(), we submit a Runnable to an ExecutorService.
- The network work runs in runSshCommand on a background thread.
- UI updates are posted back to the main thread using a Handler.

Optional: WorkManager alternative (when appropriate)
- Use WorkManager if you need the SSH command to run even if the app is backgrounded or after process death, or if you need retries/backoff/constraints (e.g., only on unmetered Wi‑Fi).
- Caveat: WorkManager is designed for deferrable work; running an interactive SSH command immediately after a button press is better served with an Executor or coroutines.
- If you choose WorkManager, create a Worker that uses JSch in doWork(), pass host/port/cmd as inputData, and enqueue with WorkManager.getInstance(context).enqueue(…).

Backward compatibility
- ExecutorService and Handler work on all API levels back to very old Android; this code runs on Android 5.0+ without additional changes.
- No need for different code paths by API level.
- WorkManager (if used) is provided by AndroidX and backports to older APIs via its library.

Potential issues and notes
- Don’t perform network operations on the main thread; the code above avoids that.
- Cancellation: Future.cancel(true) will interrupt the thread, but JSch may need an explicit channel.disconnect(); the sample checks for interruption and disconnects.
- JSch and algorithms: Some modern servers disable older ciphers. If you hit algorithm mismatches, upgrade JSch or consider sshj, which supports newer algorithms more readily.
- Timeouts: session.connect(…) has a connect timeout; long-running commands can still block. Consider implementing a command timeout if needed.
- Local network permission: Not required for raw sockets, but on Android 10+ certain Wi‑Fi scans need location permissions; your SSH socket does not.

Security and best practices
- Prefer key-based authentication over passwords. Use jsch.addIdentity with a key stored securely.
- Verify host keys. Do not permanently set StrictHostKeyChecking to "no". Maintain a known_hosts file and ship or populate it after a trusted initial connection.
- Never hard-code credentials in code or in your repo. Store secrets in encrypted form (Android Keystore + EncryptedSharedPreferences) and/or obtain them securely at runtime.
- Limit command surface area on the server. Expose only necessary commands (e.g., via a dedicated user with limited privileges). Consider wrapping scripts to validate inputs.
- Log minimally and avoid logging sensitive data (IPs, usernames, commands that contain secrets).
- If multiple commands need to run frequently, consider keeping a single session/channel open for a short period to reduce overhead, but be mindful of lifecycle and cancellation.

This approach keeps your SSH mechanism intact, replaces AsyncTask with a modern thread + handler pattern, and remains compatible across Android versions.

